<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Italian Islands of the Aegean</h1><p class="st-meta">1912 CE – 1947 CE · capital Rhodes</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Italy's Dodecanese colony, seized from the Ottomans in 1912, which left a legacy of Italian architecture before passing to Greece in 1947.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italo-turkish-war">Italo-Turkish War</a> of 1911-1912, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> and the other islands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ouchy">Treaty of Ouchy</a> in 1912 made the occupation provisional, but Italy kept the islands through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> in 1923 recognised its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Italian governors ruled from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mario-lago">Mario Lago</a> (1922-1936) invested in roads, agriculture, archaeology and buildings, including the restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-the-grand-master-rhodes">Palace of the Grand Master</a> and new Italian rationalist architecture. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-maria-de-vecchi">Cesare Maria De Vecchi</a> pursued forced Italianisation of education and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> church. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> emigrated.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy's</a> armistice in 1943 German troops took over, defeating the British at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leros">Leros</a> and deporting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in 1944. British military administration followed in 1945, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-treaties-1947">Paris Peace Treaty</a> of 1947 gave the islands to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece</a>; they were formally united in 1948.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/italian-islands-of-the-aegean.jpg" alt="Italian Islands of the Aegean" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: F l a n k e r · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Italy_(1861-1946)_crowned.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1912 CE – 1947 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rhodes</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mario Lago, Cesare Maria De Vecchi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity, Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Italian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Italian Dodecanese, Isole Italiane dell'Egeo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=italian-islands-of-the-aegean" aria-label="Italian Islands of the Aegean on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5390 864 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Italian Islands of the Aegean lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5710 1120L5709 970M5710 1120L6173 1949M5710 1120L4997 651M5710 1120L5591 1068"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0M6173 1949h0M4997 651h0M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5710 1120h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5710 1120h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rhodes. <a href="/atlas?year=1930&amp;focus=italian-islands-of-the-aegean">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
